# Abstract {.unnumbered}

This project builds and evaluates a real-time object detector that can recognise 35 categories of
objects in a camera image, as the perception layer of an assistive navigation aid. The starting
point was an annotated dataset of 16,122 images arranged in four batches. The dataset had no
class-name metadata at all, so every class had to be handled by its numeric ID (0â€“34) throughout.

A read-only audit was performed first. It found label noise, 727 groups of byte-identical images,
and 222 duplicate groups that already spanned the train, validation and test splits. A cleaned,
writable copy of the dataset was then built without modifying a single source file: 823 redundant
images were removed and cross-split duplicate groups were collapsed, leaving 15,303 images and
21,944 bounding boxes.

A YOLO11n model, pre-trained on COCO, was fine-tuned for 60 epochs at 640 Ã— 640 resolution on a
laptop RTX 4050 GPU in 1.89 hours. On the untouched test split the model reached a precision of
0.843, a recall of 0.794, mAP@50 of 0.847 and mAP@50-95 of 0.647. All 35 classes were evaluated
individually. The best weights were exported to ONNX (10.2 MB, static 640 Ã— 640, verified with the
ONNX checker) and run at roughly 0.04 s per frame on CPU. Three user-facing tools were built on top
of the model: a batch image predictor that writes an annotated image and a CSV of boxes, a class
inspection tool that crops real examples of any class ID, and a live webcam demo with on-screen
confidence and frame-rate feedback. An independent COCO detector was used to recover the meaning of
the most common class IDs as supporting evidence, since no official class names exist.

The report documents the dataset, the preparation pipeline, the model, the training run, the
evaluation results, the optimisations that were actually applied, the applications, and the honest
limitations of the result.

