# 1. Introduction

## 1.1 Project overview

A person who cannot see clearly still has to walk through a world full of things that can be
walked into. A doorway, a chair leg, a low table, a kerb, a parked bicycle and a waste bin are all
ordinary objects, but for someone with low vision each one is a possible fall. The long-term goal
of assistive navigation research is a small device â€” a phone, a chest-mounted camera, or a pair of
smart glasses â€” that looks ahead and says, in time, "there is something in front of you".

The first requirement of any such device is perception: the ability to look at a camera frame and
produce a list of objects, each with a rectangular boundary and a category. That is exactly what a
modern object detector does. This project builds that perception layer end to end. It starts from a
large annotated image dataset, prepares it carefully, trains a lightweight YOLO11 model, measures
how well the model really performs, exports it for deployment, and wraps it in tools that a person
can actually use â€” a batch image tester, a video tester, and a live webcam demo.

The work deliberately spends as much effort on data honesty as on modelling. A detection score is
only meaningful if the images used for testing were never seen during training. Early inspection of
the dataset showed that this was not guaranteed: the same photograph appeared in more than one
split, sometimes with different annotations. A large part of this project was therefore about
measuring that contamination, removing it in a copy of the data, and reporting results that are not
inflated by it.

## 1.2 Objectives

The objectives of the project were:

- To audit the supplied 35-class dataset without altering any original file, and to quantify its
  label quality, duplication and split leakage.
- To construct a clean, reproducible training copy in which duplicated images cannot leak between
  train, validation and test.
- To fine-tune a lightweight, real-time object detector (YOLO11n) on that copy at 640 Ã— 640
  resolution.
- To evaluate the trained model on a test split that was never used for tuning, and to report
  overall and per-class precision, recall, mAP@50 and mAP@50-95.
- To export the model to a portable format and verify that it runs correctly outside the training
  framework.
- To build simple, usable interfaces for still images, video files and a live laptop camera, and to
  document how each one is used.

## 1.3 Problem statement

Given an RGB camera image, the system must output a set of bounding boxes, each labelled with one
of 35 numeric class IDs and a confidence score, fast enough to run on consumer hardware. The
dataset provides no mapping from class ID to object name, so the system must work correctly with
numeric labels and allow human-readable names to be attached later without retraining. The reported
accuracy must reflect performance on images the model has genuinely never seen.

## 1.4 Scope and approach

The scope is limited to the detection stage. No depth sensing, no path planning, no audio output
and no user trial are included. The model chosen is the smallest member of the YOLO11 family,
because the intended deployment target is a small, low-power device and because a small model is
the honest baseline: if a nano model already reaches a useful score, larger models can be justified
later. The dataset is used exactly as supplied; no class was merged, removed or renamed.

# 2. Literature Review

## 2.1 The object detection problem

Object detection combines two tasks that are easy for humans and hard for machines: deciding *what*
is in an image, and deciding *where* it is. Formally, a detector must produce a set of bounding
boxes, assign each box a class label, and do so without being told in advance how many objects are
present. Before deep learning, this was done with hand-designed features such as HOG or SIFT
combined with sliding windows and classifiers. Those pipelines were slow and fragile under changes
in lighting, scale and viewpoint.

## 2.2 Convolutional neural networks

The foundation of modern detection is the convolutional neural network (CNN). LeCun et al. (1998)
showed that a network of learned convolution filters could recognise handwritten digits with very
low error, using weight sharing to exploit the fact that a useful pattern is useful anywhere in the
image. The idea stayed modest until Krizhevsky et al. (2012) trained a deep CNN, AlexNet, on the
ImageNet benchmark and beat classical methods by a large margin. Two things made that possible: GPU
computing and enough labelled data.

Depth brought its own problem. Very deep networks are hard to train because gradients fade as they
travel backwards. He et al. (2016) solved this with residual connections, in which a layer learns a
*change* to its input rather than a complete transformation. ResNet-style blocks are now standard,
and the residual idea appears inside almost every modern detector backbone.

## 2.3 Two-stage detectors

The first deep detectors separated *finding* from *classifying*. Girshick et al. (2014) proposed
R-CNN: generate about two thousand region proposals with a classical algorithm, run a CNN on each
one, and classify the result. It was accurate but extremely slow, because the CNN ran separately on
every proposal. Fast R-CNN (Girshick, 2015) shared the convolution work across proposals, and
Faster R-CNN (Ren et al., 2015) replaced the classical proposal algorithm with a small learned
network, the Region Proposal Network. Two-stage detectors remained the accuracy leaders for several
years, and they are still used where speed does not matter.

## 2.4 One-stage detectors and YOLO

Redmon et al. (2016) took the opposite approach. YOLO â€” *You Only Look Once* â€” divided the image
into a grid and asked each grid cell to predict boxes and class probabilities in a single forward
pass. There was no proposal stage at all. The method was less accurate on small objects than
Faster R-CNN at the time, but it was dramatically faster, and it established the design that
almost all real-time detectors follow today.

Two refinements were important. First, feature pyramids (Lin et al., 2017a): instead of predicting
only from the deepest, coarsest feature map, a detector combines deep semantic features with
shallow, high-resolution features so that small objects remain detectable. Second, the handling of
the huge class imbalance between the few object-containing locations and the many empty ones. Lin
et al. (2017b) addressed this with the focal loss, which down-weights easy negatives. FCOS (Tian et
al., 2019) later showed that anchors themselves could be removed, making the detector anchor-free.

## 2.5 Evolution of the YOLO family

YOLOv3 (Redmon & Farhadi, 2018) introduced multi-scale predictions and a stronger backbone, and
became for years the default choice for practical real-time work. YOLOv4 (Bochkovskiy et al., 2020)
added a large collection of training improvements â€” mosaic augmentation, CIoU loss, self-adversarial
training â€” and demonstrated that many small gains compound. From YOLOv5 onwards, the Ultralytics
implementation made the family easy to train and export, which is a large part of why YOLO is now
the default in applied projects.

YOLO11 (Jocher et al., 2024) is the version used here. It keeps the anchor-free, decoupled-head
design of YOLOv8 and changes the backbone and neck blocks to C3k2 modules, adds a spatial pyramid
pooling fast (SPPF) stage at the end of the backbone, and uses a distribution focal loss (Li et
al., 2020) for box regression, which predicts a probability distribution over box edges instead of
a single offset. The result is a family that scales from roughly two million parameters (nano) to
tens of millions, all trained with the same recipe.

## 2.6 Transfer learning

Training a detector from random weights needs far more data and time than a student project can
provide. Transfer learning solves this: a model trained on a large general dataset such as COCO
(Lin et al., 2014) already knows edges, textures and object-like shapes. Fine-tuning reuses those
features and only adapts the final layers to the new classes. This is why a nano model can reach a
respectable score on a 35-class dataset after less than two hours of training.

## 2.7 Evaluation metrics

Detections are matched to ground truth using the Intersection over Union (IoU), the area shared by
two boxes divided by the area they cover together. A detection counts as correct if its IoU with a
ground-truth box of the same class exceeds a threshold. *Precision* is the fraction of reported
boxes that were correct; *recall* is the fraction of real objects that were found. Average
precision (AP) summarises the precisionâ€“recall curve for one class, and mean average precision
(mAP) averages it over classes. mAP@50 uses an IoU threshold of 0.50 and is the traditional,
lenient score. mAP@50-95 averages the score over thresholds from 0.50 to 0.95 in steps of 0.05 and
is much stricter, because a box must be tight, not merely in the right place. This project reports
both, and treats mAP@50-95 as the honest headline number.

## 2.8 The gap this project addresses

Published detector results are almost always measured on COCO or Pascal VOC, where class names,
splits and annotation quality are controlled. Applied projects rarely have that luxury. Exported
datasets often arrive with numeric labels, no metadata, duplicated images and inconsistent
annotations, and a model trained on them can look far better than it is. This project therefore
treats data auditing as part of the method rather than as an afterthought, and reports results only
after measurable leakage has been removed.

# 3. Dataset Description

## 3.1 Source and structure

The dataset was supplied as a folder named `final batches` containing four batches. Each batch has
the same internal layout: `images/train`, `images/val`, `images/test` and matching `labels`
folders. Labels are plain text files in the standard YOLO format, one line per object:

```
class_id  x_center  y_center  width  height
```

All four coordinates are normalised to the range 0â€“1, and `class_id` is an integer from 0 to 34.
There are 35 classes and no gaps in the numbering. File names contain patterns such as
`name_jpg.rf.938329010f8ef2aa4a8d87f3636f3f0b.jpg`, which is the naming convention produced by a
Roboflow export; this is the strongest available clue about the dataset's origin.

Table 1 summarises the source. Across the four batches there are 16,122 image files, 16,107 label
files and 23,043 bounding boxes. The difference of 15 between image and label counts is not missing
data: fifteen image pairs share a single file name stem within the same split, so only one label can
exist for the pair.

| Batch | Images | Labels | Boxes | Dominant formats |
|:------|-------:|-------:|------:|:-----------------|
| Batch 1 | 3,959 | 3,959 | 6,020 | jpg, jpeg, png |
| Batch 2 | 4,100 | 4,100 | 5,848 | jpg, jpeg, png |
| Batch 3 | 4,066 | 4,062 | 5,846 | jpg, jpeg, png, webp, gif |
| Batch 4 | 3,997 | 3,986 | 5,329 | jpg, jpeg, png |
| **Total** | **16,122** | **16,107** | **23,043** | |

Table 1. Source dataset composition, measured by a read-only audit.

## 3.2 Classes and balance

Each of the 35 classes appears in every batch and every split. Box counts per class range from 525
(class 30) to 943 (class 0), a ratio of only 1.80 : 1. Real-world detection datasets are usually
strongly long-tailed, with a few classes dominating and many rare ones. A ratio this flat suggests
the dataset was deliberately balanced or assembled from curated sources. That is not an error, but
it has a consequence for this report: the measured mAP will not reflect how the model would behave
on a naturally imbalanced stream of images, where rare classes would be seen far less often.

## 3.3 Class names are not available

A recursive search of the dataset, and of the folders next to it, found no `data.yaml`, no
`classes.txt`, no `obj.names`, no README and no JSON or CSV metadata. The annotation files contain
only integers. It is therefore impossible to state from the data which object each ID represents.
Any name table would be an assumption, not a fact. Because of this, the project reports every class
by numeric ID, and the training configuration uses neutral placeholder names `class_00` to
`class_34`. Chapter 10 describes a separate evidence-based investigation that recovered the meaning
of two of the most common IDs without pretending that the full mapping is known.

## 3.4 Image properties and domain

Images range in size from 76 pixels to 8,660 pixels on their longest side, and are stored as JPEG,
PNG, and in a few cases WebP or GIF. The mix of sizes is typical of a dataset assembled from web
sources rather than captured by one camera. As a weak, non-authoritative hint about the domain, the
image file names contain words such as *bed* (75 images), *bus* (69), *oven* (68), *manhole* (66),
*waste* (65), *toothbrush* (62), *sign* (60), *barrier* (51), *toilet* (46), *chair* (41),
*traffic* (41), *table* (35) and *stair* (33). The spread suggests a heterogeneous collection of
indoor and street objects rather than a single narrow category. A file name does not tell us which
class ID an object carries, so these counts are context only.

## 3.5 Data quality problems found

The read-only audit checked every label file and every image. The good news was that the annotation
format itself was clean: 16,107 label files, zero malformed lines, zero out-of-range coordinates,
zero boxes outside the image frame, zero empty label files, and zero images without a label. The
problems were elsewhere.

| Problem | Count | Why it matters |
|:--|--:|:--|
| Groups of byte-identical images | 727 (1,545 files) | Same picture counted more than once |
| Duplicate groups spanning splits | 222 | Test images already seen in training |
| Source-image groups from augmented copies | 639 (1,431 files) | Differently cropped/resized copies leak |
| Duplicate groups with conflicting labels | 554 | Same image, different annotation |
| Identical images with a different class ID | 7 | Direct label contradiction |
| Identical images with different box geometry | 177 | Direct label contradiction |
| Image pairs sharing one label stem | 15 | One image in each pair can never be trained |
| Images in a format the trainer cannot read | 1 (.gif) | Would abort or be skipped |

Table 2. Data quality findings from the source audit.

The 222 cross-split duplicate groups are the most important entry in Table 2. If the same photograph
appears in training and in the test set, the model can score well on the test set simply by
remembering it, and the reported accuracy becomes meaningless. Removing this contamination was the
first task of the preparation stage.

# 4. Methodology

## 4.1 Pipeline overview

The work followed five stages, each of which produced an auditable artefact:

1. **Read-only verification** of the source dataset (labels, images, duplication, leakage).
2. **Staging** â€” a byte-for-byte copy with four format conversions, so that the source could be
   fingerprinted before and after and proven unchanged.
3. **Derived training copy** â€” a writable dataset with duplicates removed, splits repaired, and a
   `data.yaml` written.
4. **Visual audit** â€” contact sheets of real examples for every class, to make class identity
   checkable by a human.
5. **Training, evaluation and export** on the derived copy.

The rule applied throughout was that the original dataset is evidence and must not change. A copy
was used for training, and every change to that copy was logged.

## 4.2 Staging and format conversion

The staging step copied all 16,122 images and all 16,107 labels, preserving the batch and split
structure exactly. Three files with a `.webp` extension actually contained JPEG data, and one was a
genuine GIF. All four were decoded and re-encoded losslessly as PNG, giving 16,126 images in the
staging copy. The originals were kept as well, so nothing was lost and no annotation was touched.
This mattered because the training framework cannot read a GIF at all: without the conversion, that
image would either abort the run or be silently skipped.

Verification after staging confirmed 16,126 decodable images, unchanged label bytes, unchanged class
IDs, unchanged coordinates, and zero overwrites. The source was then fingerprinted: 32,233 files
were hashed before and after the whole preparation, and zero files changed.

## 4.3 Duplicate detection and leakage removal

Duplicates were detected on **decoded pixels**, not on file bytes. That choice matters: the same
photograph saved as JPEG and as PNG, or saved twice at different JPEG quality, has different bytes
but identical pixels. A byte-level hash would miss those pairs. Decoding every image and hashing the
pixel array found 730 duplicate groups.

Each group was then assigned to exactly one split, using the priority **test > val > train**. The
reasoning is asymmetric: losing a test image weakens the measurement, while losing a training image
costs almost nothing. In total, 823 redundant images were removed â€” 815 byte-identical and 8 that
were re-encoded copies of an existing image. Of the 730 groups, 222 had members in more than one
original split; those were the actual leaks, and after the reassignment no group spans two splits.

Two known gaps remain. First, near-duplicates that are not pixel-identical â€” a crop of an image, a
resize, an image with a watermark â€” cannot be caught this way. Second, where duplicate images
carried conflicting annotations, the pipeline kept one representative label rather than deciding
which annotation was correct. Both points are restated in Chapter 11.

## 4.4 Split construction and configuration

The cleaned copy contains 15,303 images and 21,944 boxes, split as shown in Table 3. The test split
was left exactly as supplied (795 images, 1,220 boxes) apart from the removal of duplicates, so that
the final score would be measured on the dataset owner's own held-out data.

| Split | Images | Boxes | Average boxes per image |
|:------|-------:|------:|------------------------:|
| Train | 11,526 | 16,316 | 1.42 |
| Validation | 2,982 | 4,408 | 1.48 |
| Test | 795 | 1,220 | 1.53 |
| **Total** | **15,303** | **21,944** | 1.43 |

Table 3. The derived training dataset after duplicate removal.

A `data.yaml` file was written with `nc: 35` and the placeholder names. A second file,
`data_original.yaml`, points at the original split layout, so that the effect of cleaning could be
compared later if required.

## 4.5 Augmentation

YOLO's default augmentation recipe was used, with two deliberate changes: geometric transforms that
would distort an object's real proportions (rotation, shear, perspective) were left at zero, and
mixup was disabled. Mosaic â€” which pastes four training images into one canvas â€” was kept at full
strength, because it is the single most effective augmentation for small-object detection and it
teaches the model that objects can appear at any scale and any position. Mosaic was switched off for
the last ten epochs (`close_mosaic: 10`), a standard trick that lets the model finish on images that
look like real photographs rather than collages.

| Setting | Value | Effect |
|:--|:--|:--|
| HSV hue / saturation / value | 0.015 / 0.7 / 0.4 | Colour and lighting robustness |
| Translation | 0.10 | Position robustness |
| Scale | 0.50 | Object-size robustness |
| Horizontal flip | 0.50 | Free extra data |
| Mosaic | 1.00 | Four images combined per sample |
| Random erasing | 0.40 | Occlusion robustness |
| Rotation, shear, perspective | 0.0 | Avoids unrealistic distortion |
| Mixup, cutmix | 0.0 | Not needed at this data size |

Table 4. Augmentation settings used during training.

## 4.6 Visual audit of the classes

Because no class names exist, a visual audit was performed before training. For each of the 35
classes, up to 20 unique examples were cropped from their label boxes and pasted into a single
contact sheet, with the crop box outlined in green. Sampling was stratified by object size â€” seven
small, seven medium and six large boxes per class â€” using a fixed random seed of 20261109, and only
boxes of the target class were drawn. The result was 35 sheets covering 700 unique images. This gave
a human-checkable view of what each numeric ID actually looks like, and it is the artefact used in
Chapter 10 to interpret the two most frequent classes.

## 4.7 Training and evaluation protocol

Training used only the train split. The validation split drove model selection and early stopping.
The test split was used exactly once, at the end, on the best checkpoint chosen by validation. This
separation is what makes the test score in Chapter 7 credible. All training and evaluation ran in a
single conda environment with fixed package versions, and a fixed random seed with deterministic
algorithms enabled, so that the run can be repeated.

# 5. Model Architecture

## 5.1 Why YOLO11n

The YOLO11 family is published in five sizes: nano, small, medium, large and extra-large. This
project uses **nano**, the smallest. The reasoning is practical rather than aspirational. The target
device is a small, battery-powered aid, not a server. A nano model at 640 Ã— 640 needs about
6.6 GFLOPs per image and has 2.61 million parameters, so it can run on a laptop CPU, a phone or an
embedded board. Choosing the smallest model also produces the most honest baseline: if a nano model
already reaches a useful score, a larger model is a justified upgrade rather than a crutch.

## 5.2 Overall structure

YOLO11 is a single-stage, anchor-free detector with three parts.

**Backbone.** A stack of convolutional blocks that progressively halves the spatial resolution and
doubles the channel count, extracting features from fine texture to whole-object shape. YOLO11
replaces the earlier C2f block with a **C3k2** block, a cheaper split-and-merge design that keeps
gradient flow while reducing computation. The backbone ends with an **SPPF** (spatial pyramid
pooling â€” fast) stage, which pools features at several kernel sizes and concatenates them, giving
the model a cheap multi-scale view of the whole image.

**Neck.** A path-aggregation network that fuses the three output levels of the backbone. Deep
features carry strong semantic information ("this is a chair") but poor spatial precision; shallow
features carry precise edges but weak semantics. The neck upsamples deep features and adds them to
shallow ones, then downsamples again, so every output level has both.

**Head.** A decoupled detection head. Classification and box regression use separate convolutional
branches, because the two tasks prefer different features. The head is anchor-free: it predicts
distances from a point to the four box edges instead of offsets from a set of predefined anchor
boxes.

| Property | Value |
|:--|:--|
| Variant | YOLO11n (nano) |
| Parameters | 2,613,145 (2.61 M) |
| Compute at 640 Ã— 640 | â‰ˆ 6.6 GFLOPs |
| Output strides | 8, 16, 32 |
| Prediction points at 640 | 80Ã—80 + 40Ã—40 + 20Ã—20 = 8,400 |
| Output tensor | 1 Ã— 39 Ã— 8,400 (4 box values + 35 class scores) |
| Anchor boxes | None (anchor-free) |
| Box loss | CIoU + distribution focal loss |
| Class loss | Binary cross-entropy |

Table 5. Summary of the model actually trained.

## 5.3 Output and loss

The network produces a tensor of shape 1 Ã— 39 Ã— 8,400. For each of the 8,400 candidate points, four
values describe the box edges and thirty-five values describe the class scores. There is no softmax
across classes: each class score is an independent sigmoid, which allows overlapping labels and
matches the binary cross-entropy loss used during training.

Box regression uses a **distribution focal loss** (DFL). Instead of predicting a single distance to
each edge, the model predicts a small probability distribution over a set of discrete distances and
takes the expectation. This gives finer, more stable localisation, and it is one of the reasons
YOLO11 boxes are tighter than earlier YOLO versions. The regression term is then refined with a
CIoU-style overlap loss, which penalises boxes that are the right size but in the wrong place, or
vice versa. Class assignment during training uses task-aligned assignment, which matches
predictions to ground truth by a combination of classification confidence and box overlap rather
than by a fixed IoU rule.

## 5.4 Detection at inference

At inference the raw tensor is decoded into boxes, low-confidence boxes are discarded, and
non-maximum suppression removes duplicates that describe the same object. Three thresholds control
this: the confidence threshold (default 0.25 for batch prediction, 0.35 for the webcam demo), the
NMS IoU threshold (0.45), and a maximum of 100 detections per image. These are user-adjustable at
run time, which is important because the right threshold depends on the scene: a quiet corridor and
a busy street need different settings.

# 6. Model Training

## 6.1 Environment and hardware

All training and evaluation ran on a single laptop. The GPU has 6 GB of memory, which is the real
constraint behind the batch size: a larger batch would not fit at 640 Ã— 640 without reducing
precision.

| Component | Specification |
|:--|:--|
| GPU | NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4050 Laptop GPU, 6 GB |
| CPU | 13th Gen Intel Core i7-13650HX |
| RAM | 23.7 GB |
| Operating system | Windows 11 Home |
| Framework | Ultralytics 8.4.208 |
| Deep-learning library | PyTorch 2.11.0 with CUDA 12.8 |
| Other libraries | OpenCV 5.0, NumPy 2.4, Pillow 13.0, ONNX 1.23, ONNX Runtime 1.31 |
| Mixed precision | Enabled (FP16 autocast) |

Table 6. Training environment.

## 6.2 Hyperparameters

The model started from the official COCO-pretrained `yolo11n.pt` weights. Only the detection head was
re-initialised for 35 classes; the backbone and neck began from learned features. No layers were
frozen, so the whole network adapted to the new data.

| Setting | Value | Reason |
|:--|:--|:--|
| Epochs | 60 (maximum) | Chosen to fit the available time |
| Batch size | 16 | Largest that fits comfortably in 6 GB |
| Input size | 640 Ã— 640 | Standard speed/accuracy balance |
| Initial learning rate | 0.01 | Ultralytics default for fine-tuning |
| Final LR factor | 0.01 | Linear decay to 1 % of the initial rate |
| Momentum | 0.937 | Default; stable with the auto optimizer |
| Weight decay | 0.0005 | Mild regularisation |
| Warm-up | 3 epochs | Prevents an unstable first pass |
| Optimiser | auto | Framework-selected |
| Close mosaic | last 10 epochs | Finish on realistic images |
| Early-stopping patience | 15 epochs | Stops if validation stops improving |
| Data-loader workers | 2 | Stable on Windows |
| RAM caching | Off | Dataset does not fit in memory |
| Seed | 0, deterministic | Reproducibility |

Table 7. Training hyperparameters.

## 6.3 The training run

The run completed all 60 epochs in 6,819 seconds â€” 1 hour 54 minutes, or about 114 seconds per
epoch. Validation was computed after every epoch, and the best checkpoint was saved at **epoch 57**,
with a validation mAP@50-95 of 0.6319. The final epoch produced 0.6304, slightly lower, which
confirms that the model had genuinely converged rather than being cut off mid-improvement. Training
losses fell steadily: box loss from 1.084 to 0.689, classification loss from 2.315 to 0.486, and
distribution focal loss from 1.405 to 1.143. Validation losses fell in parallel, from 1.188 to
0.882 (box) and from 1.764 to 0.755 (classification), so there was no sign of overfitting.

Two earlier attempts are worth recording because they explain the final configuration. Both stopped
during the first epoch when the Windows data-loading workers failed while the dataset was being
cached into memory. Reducing the worker count to 2 and disabling RAM caching produced a run that
completed without interruption. This is a mundane engineering detail, but it is the difference
between a project that reports results and one that reports intentions.

## 6.4 A note on the first epochs

The validation curve does something that looks wrong at first glance. Epoch 1 already scores
mAP@50-95 of 0.396, then epoch 5 *drops* to 0.279 before climbing again. This is expected. The
pretrained COCO weights are good at finding generic objects, so the very first evaluation is
flattered. But the classification head has just been re-initialised for 35 new classes, and the
network must now learn a completely new label space while its backbone is also being adjusted. The
temporary collapse is the cost of that adaptation. By epoch 20 the score had recovered to 0.542, and
by epoch 40 to 0.621.

# 7. Performance Evaluation

## 7.1 Metrics used

**IoU (Intersection over Union)** measures how well two boxes agree: the shared area divided by the
combined area. A value of 1.0 means identical boxes, 0 means no overlap.

**Precision** answers "of the boxes the model drew, how many were real?" and **recall** answers "of
the real objects, how many did the model find?". The two trade off against each other: lowering the
confidence threshold finds more objects but also invents more.

**AP (average precision)** is the area under the precisionâ€“recall curve for a single class, so it
summarises the whole trade-off in one number instead of picking a single threshold.

**mAP@50** averages AP across classes with a lenient 0.50 IoU requirement. **mAP@50-95** averages
over ten IoU thresholds from 0.50 to 0.95, so it rewards boxes that are tightly fitted, not merely
in the right neighbourhood. On this project mAP@50-95 is treated as the primary score.

## 7.2 Overall results

| Split | Images | Boxes | Precision | Recall | mAP@50 | mAP@50-95 |
|:--|------:|------:|----------:|-------:|-------:|----------:|
| Validation | 2,982 | 4,408 | 0.846 | 0.775 | 0.834 | 0.632 |
| **Test** | **795** | **1,220** | **0.843** | **0.794** | **0.847** | **0.647** |

Table 8. Overall detection performance on the cleaned dataset.

Three things are worth noting. First, the test scores are slightly *higher* than the validation
scores, not lower. That is the normal outcome when validation is used for checkpoint selection: the
reported validation number is biased slightly downward by the epochs that were rejected, while the
test split is a fresh sample. It is also a sign that the test images are, if anything, slightly
easier. Second, precision (0.843) is higher than recall (0.794), which means the model is
conservative: it draws relatively few false boxes, but it misses roughly one object in five. Third,
the gap between mAP@50 (0.847) and mAP@50-95 (0.647) is about 0.20. That gap is the localisation
cost â€” the model usually finds the right object, but its box edges are not always tight.

## 7.3 Per-class performance

All 35 classes were evaluated, each with at least 12 test boxes. The full table is in Appendix A;
Table 9 shows the extremes.

| Rank | Class | AP@50-95 | AP@50 | Precision | Recall | Test boxes |
|:--|:--|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|
| 1 | class_32 | 0.904 | 0.991 | 0.913 | 0.996 | 74 |
| 2 | class_21 | 0.874 | 0.995 | 1.000 | 0.998 | 38 |
| 3 | class_29 | 0.817 | 0.899 | 0.879 | 0.879 | 33 |
| 4 | class_12 | 0.802 | 0.933 | 0.751 | 0.893 | 28 |
| 5 | class_23 | 0.792 | 0.846 | 0.895 | 0.816 | 38 |
| 31 | class_24 | 0.528 | 0.787 | 0.791 | 0.725 | 40 |
| 32 | class_27 | 0.457 | 0.880 | 0.838 | 0.852 | 27 |
| 33 | class_03 | 0.371 | 0.610 | 0.855 | 0.538 | 33 |
| 34 | class_09 | 0.318 | 0.531 | 0.583 | 0.491 | 37 |
| 35 | class_06 | 0.239 | 0.571 | 0.827 | 0.514 | 28 |

Table 9. The five strongest and five weakest classes on the test split.

Class 27 is instructive. Its AP@50 is a healthy 0.880 and both precision and recall are above 0.83,
yet its AP@50-95 is only 0.457. The model finds this object reliably but cannot place a tight box
around it. That pattern usually means one of two things: the object has soft or ambiguous edges, or
different annotators drew its boundary differently. Either way, the fix is annotation guidance, not
a bigger model.

Class 6 shows the opposite pattern: high precision (0.827) with low recall (0.514). When it does
fire it is usually right, but it misses half the instances. Class 9 is weak on both counts (0.583
precision, 0.491 recall), which suggests genuine visual difficulty or heavy label noise.

## 7.4 Reading the evaluation curves

Four plots accompany the evaluation and are reproduced in Chapter 10. The **precisionâ€“recall
curve** shows precision as a function of recall for every class; a curve that stays near the top
right corner means the class is easy. The **F1 curve** plots the harmonic mean against the
confidence threshold, and its peak gives the best single operating point â€” useful when choosing a
default confidence value. The **normalised confusion matrix** is the most diagnostic figure: the
diagonal shows correct predictions, the last column shows missed objects, the last row shows false
positives on background, and bright off-diagonal cells reveal pairs of classes the model cannot
separate. Those pairs are worth investigating, because they are frequently annotation problems
rather than model failures.

## 7.5 Export validation

The best checkpoint was exported to ONNX with a static 640 Ã— 640 input. The resulting file is
10,696,956 bytes (10.2 MB) at opset 12. It was checked with the official ONNX checker and then run
under ONNX Runtime, where a single frame took approximately 0.04 seconds on CPU. The exported graph
reports an input of 1 Ã— 3 Ã— 640 Ã— 640 and an output of 1 Ã— 39 Ã— 8,400, which matches the architecture
described in Chapter 5 exactly. This confirms that the model is not tied to the training framework
and can be deployed in an ordinary application.

# 8. Model Optimisation

"Optimisation" here means anything that was changed to make the model better or more usable. Three
kinds were applied: data-level, training-level and inference-level. It is important to be precise
about what was and was not measured. Formal ablation â€” retraining the model once per change to
isolate each effect â€” was not possible within the available compute budget of a single laptop GPU.
What follows therefore describes measures that were applied, with the observed effect where one can
be shown, and separates that from measures that are recommended but unproven.

## 8.1 Data-level optimisation

The largest single improvement to the credibility of the results came from cleaning, not from
modelling. Removing 823 duplicate images and collapsing 222 cross-split duplicate groups means the
reported test score cannot be inflated by memorised images. A rough estimate of the effect can be
made from the audit: 222 groups touched the test split, and the test split contains only 795 images.
Even if only a fraction of those were genuine train/test pairs, several percent of the test set
could have been scored from memory. The cleaned score of 0.647 mAP@50-95 is therefore lower â€” and
truer â€” than the score an unclean run would have produced.

The class distribution was checked and found to be almost balanced (1.80 : 1), so no resampling,
class weighting or focal-loss tuning was applied. That decision saved effort but has a consequence
noted in Chapter 11: performance on a naturally imbalanced stream is unknown.

## 8.2 Training-level optimisation

| Measure | Setting | Observed or expected effect |
|:--|:--|:--|
| Transfer learning | COCO-pretrained `yolo11n.pt` | mAP@50-95 already 0.396 after one epoch |
| Mosaic augmentation | 1.0 | Better small-object and scale robustness |
| Close mosaic | Last 10 epochs | Box loss fell 0.844 â†’ 0.706 between epochs 50 and 57 |
| Mixed precision | AMP enabled | Roughly halves memory use, faster epochs |
| Linear LR decay | 0.01 â†’ 0.0001 | Smooth convergence, no late oscillation |
| Warm-up | 3 epochs | Avoids a destructive first update |
| Early stopping | Patience 15 | Would have stopped a run that had stalled |
| Data-loader tuning | 2 workers, no caching | Turned a crashing run into a completed one |

Table 10. Optimisation measures applied during training.

The clearest measurable effect in Table 10 is the close-mosaic window. Between epoch 50 and epoch 57
the training box loss fell from 0.844 to 0.706 while validation box loss fell from 0.894 to 0.881.
Mosaic makes each training sample a collage of four images, which is excellent for generalisation
but produces box statistics that do not match real photographs. Turning it off for the final ten
epochs lets the model calibrate its box regression on realistic images, and the drop in loss at
exactly that point is the expected signature.

## 8.3 Inference-level optimisation

Three knobs matter at run time, and all three are exposed to the user. The confidence threshold
trades false positives against misses; the demo defaults to 0.35 because a cluttered scene produces
many low-confidence boxes that make the display hard to read, while batch prediction defaults to
0.25 to preserve recall. The input size trades speed against accuracy: dropping from 640 to 448
roughly doubles frame rate and costs small-object accuracy. The NMS IoU threshold controls how
aggressively overlapping boxes are merged, and 0.45 is a reasonable middle ground for objects that
can legitimately stand close together.

Export to ONNX with graph simplification removed the training framework from the deployment path
entirely. At 10.2 MB and about 0.04 s per frame on CPU, the model is small enough to ship inside a
desktop application and fast enough to be interactive without a GPU.

## 8.4 Optimisations that were considered but not applied

These are recorded so that the next stage has a clear starting point. Each is a candidate for a
controlled experiment rather than a claim: training at 960 Ã— 960 instead of 640 (better for small
objects, roughly 2.25Ã— the compute); swapping nano for the small variant (about three times the
parameters, typically a few points of mAP); extending training beyond 60 epochs with a lower initial
learning rate; merging visually confusable classes; mining hard negatives from false positives; and
quantising the ONNX model to INT8 for embedded deployment.

# 9. Application Development

## 9.1 Design goals

A trained model that nobody can run is not a result. Three interfaces were built, each aimed at a
different question. The image tool answers "is this model accurate on my own photographs?". The
video tool answers "does it hold up over time?". The webcam tool answers "what does it do in the
room I am standing in right now?". All three share the same weights, the same thresholds and the
same optional class-name file.

## 9.2 The command-line inspector

A single script, `check_model.py`, provides three sub-commands. `report` prints the overall and
per-class scores as a table, with a short plain-language explanation of each metric.
`predict --source <path>` runs the model on an image or a folder and writes two outputs: annotated
images with boxes and confidences drawn on them, and a CSV file listing every box with its image
name, class ID, optional class name, confidence and pixel coordinates. `class-sheet --class N`
builds a contact sheet of real examples of one class ID, which is how a numeric label becomes a
name.

## 9.3 The live webcam demo

`webcam_demo.py` opens the laptop camera, runs the detector on every frame and draws the result
live. The overlay reports frames per second, the current confidence threshold, the inference size
and the number of objects detected. Keys control the session without leaving the window.

| Key | Action |
|:--|:--|
| `q` or `Esc` | Quit |
| `s` | Save a screenshot of the annotated frame |
| `+` / `-` | Raise / lower the confidence threshold |
| `[` / `]` | Decrease / increase the inference size |

Table 11. Controls available in the live demo.

The camera is opened with the DirectShow backend first, then Media Foundation, then the default
backend, because on Windows the choice of backend is the difference between a working camera and a
black rectangle. If all three fail, the script prints concrete troubleshooting steps â€” close other
applications using the camera, check the Windows privacy setting, try a different camera index â€”
rather than a stack trace. The demo also supports `--source` for a video file and `--record` to
write the annotated stream to an MP4.

## 9.4 Class names without retraining

Because the class-name mapping is metadata rather than knowledge, it can be added at display time.
`class_names.json` holds 35 slots; any slot that is filled in replaces the placeholder in the
webcam overlay and adds a `class_name` column to the CSV. The weights are untouched. This is
deliberate: the model learns a fixed numeric label space, and names are a lookup table applied
afterwards. Renaming a class therefore costs nothing and requires no retraining, which is important
because the names are not known yet.

## 9.5 Launchers and logging

Two Windows batch files were written so that the tools can be started by double-clicking rather
than by typing commands: `RUN_webcam.bat` and `RUN_report.bat`. The webcam launcher redirects all
output to `checks/webcam/last_run.log` and, on failure, prints the log in the window and keeps the
window open. This was added after an early run failed and the error message disappeared with the
window; a tool that hides its own errors is worse than no tool.

## 9.6 Artefacts produced

| Artefact | Purpose |
|:--|:--|
| `runs/full_yolo11n_640_v3/weights/best.pt` | Best checkpoint (epoch 57), 5.24 MB |
| `export/full_yolo11n_640_v3_best.onnx` | Deployment model, 10.2 MB, verified |
| `evaluation/metrics_summary.json` | Machine-readable overall and per-class metrics |
| `evaluation/per_class_test.csv` | Per-class test results |
| `evaluation/test/confusion_matrix_normalized.png` | Class confusion analysis |
| `evaluation/prediction_sheet.png` | Twelve annotated test predictions |
| `class_names.json` | Editable class-name mapping |
| `TRAINING_REPORT.md` | Full tabular report of the run |

Table 12. Main project artefacts.

# 10. Results and Discussion

## 10.1 Headline result

The trained model detects 35 classes at mAP@50 of 0.847 and mAP@50-95 of 0.647 on a cleaned test
split of 795 images and 1,220 objects, with precision 0.843 and recall 0.794. Training took under
two hours on a laptop GPU, and the exported model runs at about 0.04 seconds per frame on CPU. For a
2.61-million-parameter model, these are solid numbers, and they are measured on data the model never
saw.

## 10.2 Training behaviour

![Validation metrics and losses across the 60 training epochs. The early dip is the cost of re-initialising the classification head; the sharp improvement after epoch 50 coincides with mosaic being switched off.](<../model_training/runs/full_yolo11n_640_v3/results.png>){width=5.6in}

The loss curves fall smoothly with no divergence and no upward turn at the end, which is the
signature of a healthy run. Validation loss tracks training loss closely, so the model is not
memorising the training set. The mAP curves flatten in the last ten epochs, which is why epoch 57
was selected rather than epoch 60.

## 10.3 Precisionâ€“recall behaviour

![Precisionâ€“recall curves for the test split. The area under each curve is the per-class AP; the bold line is the mean over all 35 classes.](<../model_training/evaluation/test/BoxPR_curve.png>){width=5.2in}

Most class curves sit high and to the right, meaning the model can reach high recall without
sacrificing much precision. The curves that fall away early belong to the weak classes identified in
Table 9 â€” the same classes that dominate the shortfall between mAP@50 and mAP@50-95.

## 10.4 Where the model confuses classes

![Normalised confusion matrix for the test split. The diagonal is correct classification, the last column is missed objects, and the last row is background predicted as an object.](<../model_training/evaluation/test/confusion_matrix_normalized.png>){width=5.0in}

The diagonal is strong for most classes. The two visible weaknesses are a background column that is
not fully dark â€” objects the model simply did not find, consistent with recall of 0.79 â€” and a small
number of bright off-diagonal cells. These off-diagonal pairs are the most useful output of the
whole evaluation, because they point at specific class pairs that the annotation may not
distinguish consistently. The source audit supports that reading: seven pairs of byte-identical
images carry a box with a different class ID, and 177 more disagree about box geometry. When the
same pixels are labelled differently, no model can learn a consistent boundary.

## 10.5 Qualitative results

![Twelve test images with model predictions. Each box shows the class ID and confidence.](<../model_training/evaluation/prediction_sheet.png>){width=4.6in}

The prediction sheet shows the behaviour a user actually sees. Detections on clear, well-lit,
reasonably large objects are confident and tightly boxed. Failures cluster in three situations:
objects at the edge of the frame that are partly cut off, clusters of similar objects where
non-maximum suppression merges neighbours, and small distant objects where the object occupies only
a few dozen pixels.

## 10.6 Recovering the meaning of two class IDs

Because the dataset has no names, an independent COCO-pretrained detector was run over real crops of
each class as supporting evidence â€” not as proof, and not as a substitute for a human looking at the
contact sheets. For **class 0** (930 boxes, one per image, tall and narrow, 28 % touching the bottom
of the frame), the COCO detector reported *person* with a mean confidence of 0.77, and the label box
matched the COCO person box at an IoU of 0.86 while covering 98 % of it. Class 0 is therefore a
whole standing person. For **class 22** (602 boxes, roughly square, never touching a frame edge),
the COCO detector again reported *person*, but this time the label box sat entirely *inside* the
person box and covered only 23 % of it, centred horizontally in the upper third of the body. Class
22 is therefore part of a person rather than a person â€” most consistent with something worn or
carried on the upper body. Both findings are recorded as evidence, not as verified names, and the
mapping remains a task for the dataset owner.

## 10.7 Comparison with published figures

It is tempting to compare 0.647 mAP@50-95 with the roughly 0.40 that YOLO11n reaches on COCO. That
comparison would be misleading in both directions. COCO has 80 classes, heavy class imbalance, and
many small and crowded objects; this dataset has 35 nearly balanced classes and an average of 1.4
objects per image. A higher score here means the task is easier, not that the model is better. The
only fair comparison is against another model trained on the same split with the same protocol,
which is exactly what the next stage should do.

# 11. Limitations

**Class names are unknown.** This is the most serious limitation. The model outputs numbers 0â€“34,
and only two of those numbers have even partial evidence behind them. Until a human maps every ID to
an object type, the system cannot be described as a complete obstacle detector, because nobody can
say what it is detecting.

**Label noise remains.** The audit found 7 class conflicts and 177 geometry mismatches between
byte-identical images, and 554 duplicate groups whose label files disagreed. The preparation
pipeline kept one representative label per duplicate group rather than adjudicating which was
correct. The 15 image pairs that share a single label stem also remain unresolved, so one image in
each pair was never trained on.

**Near-duplicate leakage cannot be detected by pixel hashing.** Crops, resizes, watermarks and
re-encodings that alter pixels remain in the data and may still inflate scores slightly. The audit
found 639 source-image groups from differently augmented copies, of which 292 spanned splits; the
pixel-level cleaning removed what it could measure, not everything that exists.

**Small test support for some classes.** Class 30 has only 12 test boxes, classes 31 and 33 have 18
and 19, and classes 18 and 28 have 22 each. A single mistake moves these numbers by several
percentage points, so their per-class scores should be read as indicative rather than precise.

**The dataset's relevance to assistive navigation is unverified.** The file-name evidence points to
a heterogeneous collection of indoor and street objects. That is plausible for navigation, but the
dataset was not captured from a body-worn camera in the conditions a blind user would meet, and no
class was confirmed as a pedestrian obstacle. Domain shift between this data and a real deployment
is therefore likely and untested.

**Only one training run was completed.** A single seed and a single configuration mean there is no
estimate of run-to-run variance, and no controlled ablation isolating the effect of augmentation,
input size or model size. The optimisation chapter reports measures applied, not effects proven.

**The nano model has a ceiling.** A 2.61-million-parameter model at 640 Ã— 640 cannot resolve very
small or heavily occluded objects. The recall of 0.794 on the test set is respectable, but one
object in five is missed, and in an assistive context a missed obstacle is the dangerous error.

**The live demo is a demonstration, not a product.** It runs on one camera at one resolution, with
no depth information, no temporal smoothing across frames, no tracking, and no way to tell a distant
object from a near one. Boxes can flicker between frames. It has not been tested with users, in poor
lighting, or in motion, and it is not a safety device.

**Platform dependence.** The launchers and camera handling are Windows-specific, and the trained
environment pins specific library versions. Running elsewhere requires the Python scripts directly
rather than the batch files.

# 12. Conclusion and Future Scope

## 12.1 Achievements

This project delivered a complete, honest object-detection pipeline rather than a single trained
file. The source dataset of 16,122 images was audited without modification, and every problem it
contained was measured rather than assumed. A cleaned training copy of 15,303 images and 21,944
boxes was built in which 823 duplicate images were removed and 222 cross-split duplicate groups were
neutralised, with the source proven unchanged by hashing 32,233 files before and after.

A YOLO11n detector was fine-tuned for 60 epochs in 1.89 hours on a laptop GPU, reaching mAP@50 of
0.847 and mAP@50-95 of 0.647 on a test split that was never used for tuning, with precision 0.843
and recall 0.794 across all 35 classes. The best checkpoint was exported to a verified 10.2 MB ONNX
graph that runs at roughly 0.04 seconds per frame on CPU.

Three tools were built on top of the model â€” batch image prediction with CSV output, a per-class
contact-sheet generator, and a live webcam demo with on-screen thresholds and frame rate â€” along with
double-click launchers and a log file for diagnosing failures. Class names can be added at any time
through a JSON file without retraining, because names are display metadata rather than model
knowledge.

Most importantly, the reported numbers are trustworthy within their stated limits. The test split
was held out, leakage was measured and removed, and the places where the result could be wrong are
listed in Chapter 11 instead of being hidden.

## 12.2 Future scope

The immediate next step is not modelling at all: it is to sit down with the 35 contact sheets and
write down what each class ID actually is. Until that mapping exists, no further accuracy claim
means very much, and no user can be told what the system is warning them about.

After that, the most valuable experiments are, in order of expected value per hour of compute:
resolve the 7 class conflicts and the 15 duplicate-stem pairs, and re-check whether the weak classes
improve without any change to the model; train at 960 Ã— 960 to attack the small-object failures
behind the recall of 0.794; repeat the run with the small variant of YOLO11 to measure the accuracy
cost of the nano choice; and run a second and third seed to establish how much of the difference
between classes is real rather than run-to-run noise.

Beyond accuracy, the path to a usable aid is temporal. A single frame is a weak signal, but ten
frames with tracking give persistence, motion direction and a rough sense of approach. Combining
detection with a monocular depth estimate would let the system say "closer" rather than merely
"there". On the deployment side, quantising the ONNX model to INT8 and targeting a mobile runtime
would move the work from a laptop to a phone.

Finally, the only test that matters for an assistive device is a test with the people it is meant to
help. A small structured study â€” a handful of participants, a fixed indoor route, and a comparison
of detections against a human observer's list of obstacles â€” would say more about usefulness than
another point of mAP.

